course: world
lesson: 41
title: Maps, plans and the compass
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A map is a drawing of a place as a bird would see it from high above, made much smaller so that it fits on paper.
explain: A plan is a map of a small place, such as a room, a classroom or a garden: the bed, the table and the door are drawn as shapes seen from above.
explain: The scale tells how much smaller the map is: if 1 centimetre on the map stands for 100 metres on the ground, then 3 centimetres on the map mean 300 metres of real walking.
explain: The legend, or key, explains the signs of the map: a blue line for a river, a small black square for a house, a green patch for a forest.
explain: On most maps north is at the top, south at the bottom, east on the right and west on the left; a compass helps to find north outside, because its magnetic needle turns to point north.
explain: For example, a plan of your room drawn at a scale of 1 to 50 shows a 2-metre bed as a rectangle 4 centimetres long.
fact: A map shows a place from above and much smaller than it really is.
fact: A plan is a detailed map of a small place, such as a room or a school yard.
fact: The scale of a map tells how many real metres or kilometres one centimetre on the map stands for.
fact: The legend, or key, explains what each sign and colour on the map means.
fact: The four main directions are north, south, east and west; between them lie north-east, south-east, south-west and north-west.
fact: On most maps north is at the top and east is on the right.
fact: A compass needle is a small magnet that turns to point north, because the Earth itself acts like a giant magnet.
fact: A globe is a small round model of the Earth that shows the true shapes of the lands and seas.
fact: The equator is an imaginary line around the middle of the Earth, halfway between the North Pole and the South Pole.
fact: On a physical map, blue shows water, green shows low land and brown shows hills and mountains.
exercise: What is a map? | A drawing of a place seen from above and made smaller | A photograph of a place taken from the side | A list of the names of towns and rivers | A drawing of a place made bigger than it is
exercise: What is a plan of a room? | A drawing of the room and its things seen from above | A drawing of one wall of the room from the front | A list of everything that stands in the room | A drawing of the room as seen from the doorway
exercise: What does the scale of a map tell you? | How many real metres one centimetre on the map stands for | How old the map is and who drew it | Which direction is north on the map | What each colour on the map means
exercise: What does the legend, or key, of a map explain? | What the signs and colours mean | How far apart two towns really are | Which way the compass needle points | How large the whole country is in all
exercise: Where is north on most maps? | At the top | At the bottom | On the right | On the left
exercise: Which direction is on the right side of most maps? | East | West | North | South
exercise: Why does a compass needle point north? | It is a magnet turned by the Earth's magnetism | The wind always blows it towards the north | The Sun pulls it towards the north | It is heavier at one end than the other
exercise: Which direction lies halfway between north and east? | North-east | South-east | North-west | South-west
exercise: What is a globe? | A round model of the Earth | A flat map of a single country | A plan of a city seen from above | A drawing of the stars at night
exercise: What is the equator? | An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth | A real wall built across the middle of the Earth | The line that joins the North Pole to the South Pole | The border between Europe and Asia
exercise: On a physical map, what colour usually shows mountains? | Brown | Blue | Green | Yellow
exercise: On a map, 1 centimetre stands for 1 kilometre. How far apart are two villages 4 centimetres apart on the map? | 4 kilometres | 1 kilometre | 40 kilometres | 4 metres
exercise: What colour usually shows rivers, lakes and seas on a map? | Blue | Brown | Green | Red
exercise: Which direction is opposite to west? | East | North | South | South-west
exercise: Why does a map need a scale? | To work out real distances | To show which countries are the largest | To make the map look more colourful | To tell where the compass is kept
test: Dana draws her bedroom as if she were a fly on the ceiling. What has she drawn? | A plan of her bedroom | A picture of the bedroom wall | A legend of her bedroom | A globe of her bedroom
test: You face north and turn so that north is on your right hand. Which way are you facing now? | West | East | South | North-east
test: A map has the scale 1 centimetre = 10 kilometres. A road is 7 centimetres long on the map. How long is the real road? | 70 kilometres | 17 kilometres | 7 kilometres | 700 kilometres
test: On a map you see a little picture of a tree repeated many times, and you do not know what it means. Where should you look? | In the legend of the map | At the scale of the map | At the compass rose | On the back of the globe
test: Which is true about a compass? | Its needle points north almost anywhere on Earth | Its needle points towards the nearest sea | Its needle points towards the Sun at noon | Its needle only works during the daytime
test: A town is at the top of the map and a lake at the bottom. In which direction is the lake from the town? | South | North | East | West
test: Why can a globe show the shapes of the continents more truly than a flat map? | Because the Earth is round like the globe | Because a globe is always bigger than a map | Because a globe uses more colours than a map | Because a globe is drawn from the side
test: A plan of a classroom uses the scale 1 centimetre = 1 metre. The classroom is 8 metres long. How long is it on the plan? | 8 centimetres | 1 centimetre | 80 centimetres | 8 metres
test: On a physical map a large area is coloured dark brown. What is most likely there? | High mountains | A deep sea | A low green plain | A big lake
test: map says 1 cm is 1 km, school's 3 cm away, how far really | About 3 kilometres | About 3 metres | About 1 kilometre | About 30 kilometres
test: whats the little box with symbols in the corner of the map for | It is the key, explaining what the signs and colours mean | It shows how old the map is | It is where the map was printed | It lists the people who live there
test: my bro says a compass points north cos of the North Star. true? | No, its needle is a magnet pulled by the Earth's magnetism | Yes, the needle sees the North Star | Yes, but only at night | No, it points to the nearest sea
test: Lily says brown on a map means desert. right? | No, on a physical map brown shows hills and mountains | Yes, brown always means sand | Yes, and green means grass | No, brown is used for the cities, roads and big towns
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term legend, or key, of a map? | The explanation of each sign and colour upon the map | The ratio of the distance upon the map to the real distance | The direction of north upon the map | The title and date of the map
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the needle of a compass points north. | The needle is a magnet and the Earth acts as a giant magnet | The needle is drawn towards the Sun | The needle is heavier at its northern end | The wind turns the needle northward
exam: It is given that a map is oriented in the customary manner. Ascertain the direction lying to the right. | East | West | North | South
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning colours upon a physical map. | Blue denotes water, green low land, and brown hills | Blue denotes cold land, green forest, and brown desert | Blue denotes sky, green water, and brown cities | Blue denotes mountains, green sea, and brown rivers
